<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#transhumance">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Transhumance</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Transhumance is the seasonal movement of livestock and herders between different pastures, typically between lowland winter grazing and highland summer grazing. It was common in mountainous regions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">ancient Near East</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a> practised transhumance <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pastoralism">pastoralism</a> alongside sedentary farming, creating a diversified economy resilient to climatic variability.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/transhumance.jpg" alt="Transhumance" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CamDib · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Transumanza_nel_Parc_National_des_Ecrins.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Transhumance is a type of mobile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pastoralism">pastoralism</a> in which livestock are driven in herds between several fixed pastures with the seasons. It differs from sedentary pastoralism, where animals stay on one fenced area all year, and from nomadic pastoralism, where animals are moved irregularly in search of pasture. In mountain regions it means moving between high summer pastures and lowland valleys in winter, called vertical transhumance. Movement across the same plains or plateaus is horizontal transhumance.</p><p>Usually only the animals and a small number of herders travel, living in camps or huts at the grazing sites, while most of the community lives in permanent settlements. Nomadic pastoralists, by contrast, move whole communities with their herds in portable shelters such as tents and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yurt">yurts</a>. The milk, butter, yogurt and cheese of the herds may form much of the diet of such societies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word comes from French and derives from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> trans, across, and humus, ground. Transhumance developed on every inhabited continent and was practised before recorded history; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, isotope studies of livestock bones suggest that certain animals were moved seasonally. Many languages have words for the high summer pastures, often used as place names, such as hafod in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, shieling in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, and Alm or Alp in German-speaking regions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> farm workers spent the summer at a hillside hafod and returned in late autumn to the main house, the hendref. In Ireland the practice was called booleying, after the buaile, the summer pasture; it is mentioned in the Brehon Laws and was widespread in the west until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. In southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> drovers' roads called tratturi, up to 100 metres wide and more than 100 kilometres long, were regulated by law, with a mounted police force as far back as the 17th century. Spanish transhumance is the origin of the cowboys of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and the gauchos of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> herds went to the high mountains from late May or early June to early October, a system that evolved in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and lasted into the 20th century. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a>, Sarakatsani, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a>, Aromanians and Yörük spent summers in the mountains and winters on lowland plains. The Bhotiya of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttarakhand">Uttarakhand</a> practised it until the closure of the Sino-Indian border after the war of 1962. Colonists began summer grazing in the Australian Alps in the 1830s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Transhumance” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its economy rested on grain farming, wine and the wool of merino sheep, moved along long <b>transhumance</b> routes under the privileged guild of the Mesta and exported to Flanders and Italy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Pastoralism based on <b>transhumance</b>, practised alongside settled farming, made the economy resilient in a demanding highland environment.</blockquote><a href="/empire/median-empire">Median Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Richtersveld is today the only place where <b>transhumance</b> linked to Khoe culture continues to any great extent.</blockquote><a href="/culture/khoekhoe">Khoekhoe · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Cattle and horses were signs of wealth and prestige; sheep and pigs were kept in large numbers, and place names suggest that <b>transhumance</b> was common.</blockquote><a href="/culture/picts">Picts · Society</a></li></ul></section>
